#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056030 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}x = 8\n$$\n\nThis equation has a **fractional coefficient** of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nThe term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}x\n$$\n\nmeans that $x$ is being multiplied by $2$ and divided by $3$.\n\nWe can solve this problem by applying the multiplication principle, twice. First, we multiply both sides by ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{2}{3}x &= 8 \\\\[5pt] {\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot \\dfrac{2}{3}x &={\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot 8 \\\\[5pt] \\cancel{\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{\\cancel{3}}x &={\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot 8 \\\\ 2x &=24 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by ${\\color{red}{2}}$ to isolate $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x &=24 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{2x}{{\\color{red}{2}}} &=\\dfrac{24}{{\\color{red}{2}}} \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{\\color{red}{2}}} &=\\dfrac{24}{{\\color{red}{2}}} \\\\ x &=12 \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8576ba10-7bde-590e-9219-705766ef6f6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8150 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056031 :tutorial/content "When solving equations with fractional coefficients, there is a shortcut! We can complete division and multiplication in a single step by multiplying both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of the fractional coefficient.\n\nFor example, to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x}{5}= 6\n$$\n\nwe first express the fraction as a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fractional coefficient is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\n, and its reciprocal is\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 2 5\\right)^{-1} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}}\n$$\n\nBecause the product of a fraction and its reciprocal is equal to $1$, we can multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal to isolate $x$:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6 \\\\[5pt] \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{5}}_{\\large =\\, 1} x &= {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}} \\cdot 6 \\\\[5pt] 1 x &= \\dfrac{5\\cdot 6}{2} \\\\[5pt] x &= \\dfrac{30}{2} \\\\[5pt] x &= 15 \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "278b0f64-5aa2-525c-b86d-91239623e85b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8151 :tutorial/title "Using the Reciprocal"}]}
